# Deepcore — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Deepcore carries a debugging and automation surface, `window.__deepcore`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without touching a keyboard or waiting
on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__deepcore` as soon as the game has
initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Deepcore carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven and
inspected from code without touching a keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from `engine.debug`,
and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the page. Every scenario
driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly as
specified here. `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type, `DeepcoreDebugApi`.
{{else}}
Deepcore carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven and
inspected from code without touching a keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
game instance's `initialize` returns the finished surface. The engine holds it and
returns it from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is
installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through
it, so it is present and exactly as specified here. `src/game.ts` declares and
exports its type, `DeepcoreDebugApi`.
{{/if}}

Each operation is a reading, a pose of a single value, or one of the panel actions
below. A pose arranges the world, and the game's own physics, drill, hazard,
economy, and save rules run from there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario
driven from code behaves exactly like one played by hand.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
The debug overlay is off until it is toggled.

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated
against the delta time the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Every random choice the game makes runs off a generator
  seeded by `reset`, above all the mine's generation: the ore scatter, the hazard
  and boulder placement, and the two material nodes' positions. Reseeding and
  replaying the same calls reproduces the same mine and the same outcome exactly.
  The Quantum Teleporter's height and speed draws are the one exception, and they
  need not be reproducible from the seed.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay belongs to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the surface carries
no operation for it. That layer draws and toggles the overlay, and the game
registers what it shows.

The clock and the keyboard are the exception, because nothing outside this build
owns them. The surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it,
through `setAutoStep` and `advance`, and what injects key presses, through
`keyDown`, `keyUp`, and `press`.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The
engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives
the actions the game registers, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The
engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives
the actions the game registers, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Deepcore's, under Diagnostics below.

## Signatures

The surface carries `version` (`DEEPCORE_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Every argument and every returned value is a plain number,
string, boolean, or JSON-serializable object. Positions and velocities are in the
world units of the mine, and `col` and `row` are cell coordinates in its grid.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each operation takes the parameters its row names and acts on the live game the
moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it read and
changes nothing.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<DeepcoreState>`, followed by the parameters its row names. A pose
returns the next `DeepcoreState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read.
{{else}}
Each operation takes the parameters its row names and acts on the live game the
moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it read and
changes nothing.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The clock

### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's own
physics, drill, hazards, and timers produce the result and the canvas reflects it.
Every action held through `keyDown` drives the miner across those frames exactly
as a player holding the key would.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time
and reach the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing.

## Injecting input

`keyDown(code)` presses a key down, `keyUp(code)` releases one, and `press(code)`
is a `keyDown` immediately followed by a `keyUp`. `code` is a
`KeyboardEvent.code`, and injected keys flow through the same handling the real
keyboard feeds, so they exercise the game's own key bindings rather than a
parallel path. A key left down stays held, so a movement or thrust key
drives the miner through the game's own update across every frame `advance` runs.

Injecting input never changes `autoStep`.

{{/if}}
## Readings

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `snapshot()` | The whole observable state, as the object under Snapshot shape below. |
| `tileAt(col, row)` | One cell's observable state, as the object under Tile reads below. |
| `findTile(kind)` | The `{ col, row }` of a cell of that kind, nearest to the miner where several exist, or `null` where there is none. `kind` is one of the tile kinds below. |
| `buildings()` | One entry per surface building, `{ id, x, y, w, h }`, with `id` the building's id and `x, y, w, h` its footprint in world units. |

Every reading is pure and changes nothing.

## Poses

Each pose sets one value and nothing else. Every value a pose sets is reported by
`snapshot` or by `tileAt`, so a pose can be verified by setting a value and reading
it back.

| Operation | Sets |
| --- | --- |
| `reset(seed)` | The whole game back to the title screen, discarding any expedition in progress, and seeds the generator with `seed`, a number. Nothing else survives it. |
| `setScreen(screen)` | The current screen, to one of the screen names below. |
| `setPanel(panel)` | The open panel, to one of the panel ids below, or `null` to close whatever is open. |
| `setMode(mode)` | The expedition's mode, `"standard"` or `"hardcore"`. |
| `setWorldSize(size)` | The expedition's world size, `"quick"`, `"standard"`, or `"marathon"`. |
| `generateMine()` | Generates a fresh mine from the current seed at the current world size, and places the miner at its spawn. |
| `setMinerPosition(x, y)` | The miner's position, in world units. It moves the miner alone and changes no cell, so a miner placed inside solid rock stays lodged there until the real physics resolves it. A scenario that needs open space opens the cell itself first. |
| `setMinerVelocity(vx, vy)` | The miner's velocity, in world units per second. |
| `setFacing(facing)` | The miner's facing, `"east"` or `"west"`. |
| `setFuel(value)` | The miner's fuel, clamped to the current maximum. |
| `setHull(value)` | The miner's hull, clamped to the current maximum. |
| `setCredits(value)` | The Credits balance. |
| `setTier(track, tier)` | One upgrade track's tier, through the real upgrade path, so the maxima and the granted capacity follow as a purchase's would. `track` is one of the seven track names. |
| `setCargo(ore, count)` | The number of units of one ore or gemstone held in the cargo bay, ignoring the slot cap so a scenario can pose any load. |
| `setMaterial(material, count)` | The number of one exotic material held in the satchel, `"resonite"` or `"cryenite"`. |
| `setItemCount(item, count)` | The number of one field supply held, by its id. |
| `setTile(col, row, kind)` | One cell's kind, to `"rock"`, `"gas"`, `"lava"`, `"stone"`, `"bedrock"`, `"tunnel"`, or `"core"`, at that band's full health. This is how a scenario opens a cell, and how it removes what its requirement is not about. |
| `setOreTile(col, row, ore)` | One cell to an ore vein holding the named ore or gemstone, at its band's full health. |
| `setMaterialTile(col, row, material)` | One cell to a material node holding the named material, at its band's full health. |
| `setTileHealth(col, row, health)` | One minable cell's remaining health, so a scenario can pose a partly cut tile. |
| `setRocketInstalled(count)` | How many rocket components are installed, `0` through `5`, taken in order. |
| `setCoreTimer(seconds)` | The seconds left on a live Core Sample's timer. |
| `clearSave()` | Deletes the save slot. |
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
| `setMuted(muted)` | The audio mute toggle, the same one the mute control flips. |
{{/if}}

## Actions

These invoke the game's own actions through the same code a player's click or
keypress reaches, rather than posing an outcome. Each is the counterpart of a
control the player operates with the mouse.

| Operation | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `dropOre(ore)` | Discards one unit of that ore from the cargo bay, as the inventory's drop control does. |
| `sell()` | Sells the cargo at the Ore Market. |
| `buyFuel(units)` | Buys that many units of fuel at the Fuel Depot. |
| `buyRepair(points)` | Buys that many points of hull repair at the Fuel Depot. |
| `buyUpgrade(track)` | Buys the next tier on that track at the Upgrade Shop. |
| `buyItem(item)` | Buys one field supply of that id at the Supply Depot. |
| `useItem(item)` | Uses one field supply of that id. |
| `takeCoreSample()` | Extracts a Core Sample as drilling the Core does, banking it and starting its timer. |
| `jettison()` | Drops a carried Core Sample onto the miner's cell as a ground item. |
| `fabricate()` | Fabricates the next uninstalled rocket component at the Launch Pad. |
| `launch()` | Launches, once all five components are installed. |
| `save()` | Saves the expedition, as activating the Save Pad does, and honours the refusal while a Core Sample's timer runs. |

An action that the game's own rules refuse changes nothing, exactly as the control
it stands for would.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "mode-select" | "size-select" | "how-to-play"
        | "in-mine" | "paused" | "victory" | "game-over",
  panel: null | "fuel-depot" | "ore-market" | "upgrade-shop"
       | "supply-depot" | "launch-pad" | "inventory",
  mode: "standard" | "hardcore",
  worldSize: "quick" | "standard" | "marathon",
  coreRow: <number>,            // the deepest row at the current world size
  menuIndex: <number>,          // the screen's highlighted menu item, from 0
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,          // false while the driver clocks the game
{{/if}}
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,            // accumulated game time, in seconds
  hasSave: <boolean>,
  credits: <number>,
  creditsEarned: <number>,      // the running total earned this expedition
  depthMeters: <number>,
  deepestDepthMeters: <number>,
  coreTimer: <number> | null,   // seconds left on a live Core Sample, else null
  coreGround: null | { col: <number>, row: <number> },
  camera: { x: <number>, y: <number> },
  miner: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,   // the top-left of the miner's box, in world units
    vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
    col: <number>, row: <number>,
    facing: "east" | "west",
    state: "idle" | "walk" | "drill-down" | "drill-side"
         | "jetpack" | "fall" | "hurt" | "fuel-out",
    grounded: <boolean>,
    fuel: <number>, maxFuel: <number>,
    hull: <number>, maxHull: <number>,
    overloaded: <boolean>,
    drilling: null | { col: <number>, row: <number>,
                       dir: "down" | "left" | "right", progress: <number> },
  },
  cargo: {
    slotsUsed: <number>, slotCap: <number>,
    loadKg: <number>,
    liftLimitKg: <number>,
    ore: { <oreName>: <count> },   // held ore and gemstones, nonzero entries only
  },
  satchel: {
    resonite: <number>, cryenite: <number>,
    coreSample: <boolean>,
  },
  tiers: {
    fuel: <number>, drill: <number>, cargo: <number>, hull: <number>,
    jetpack: <number>, radiator: <number>, scanner: <number>,
  },
  items: {
    "dynamite": <number>, "plastic-explosives": <number>,
    "quantum-teleporter": <number>, "matter-transmitter": <number>,
    "nanobots": <number>, "emergency-fuel": <number>,
  },
  rocket: {
    installed: [ <componentId> ],       // in order: "hull-frame", "fuel-cells",
                                        // "guidance", "thruster", "ignition"
    nextComponent: <componentId> | null,
  },
  scanner: {
    locked: <boolean>,
    target: "resonite" | "cryenite" | null,
    dirX: <number>, dirY: <number>,     // a unit direction to the target
    distanceTiles: <number> | null,
  },
  summary: null | {
    deepestDepthMeters: <number>, creditsEarned: <number>,
    elapsedSeconds: <number>, mode: "standard" | "hardcore",
    componentsInstalled: <number>,
    deathCause: "fuel-out" | "hull-destroyed" | "core-detonation" | null,
  },
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. A field the current
screen does not use reports its resting value rather than going missing:
`summary` is `null` until a run ends, `coreTimer` and `coreGround` are `null`
while no Sample is live, `panel` is `null` while no panel is open, and
`menuIndex` rests at `0` on `in-mine`.

Three fields are derived rather than stored: `depthMeters` and
`deepestDepthMeters` from the miner's position, `overloaded` from `loadKg` against
`liftLimitKg`, and `drilling.progress`, which runs `0` to `1` as the target cell's
health drains. `scanner.locked` is `false`, with the direction zero and the
distance `null`, whenever nothing needed is in range, matching the indicator being
hidden.

## Tile reads

The grid is large, so it is read a cell at a time rather than dumped whole.
`tileAt(col, row)` returns:

```js
{
  kind: "rock" | "ore" | "material" | "gas" | "lava"
      | "stone" | "bedrock" | "tunnel" | "core",
  band: "topsoil" | "rockbed" | "deepstone" | "coreshell" | null,
  ore: <oreName> | null,
  material: "resonite" | "cryenite" | null,
  health: <number> | null,
  maxHealth: <number> | null,
}
```

`band` is `null` for a cell outside the four bands. `ore` is set on an ore cell and
`material` on a material node, and both are `null` otherwise. `health` and
`maxHealth` are `null` for a cell that is not minable. A cell outside the grid
reads as `bedrock` with every other field `null`.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as diagnostic
sources. Register at least the current screen and panel, the mode and world size,
the miner's position, velocity, state, facing, and grounded flag, its fuel and
hull against their maxima, any active drill target and its progress, the Credits,
the depth in metres, the cargo's slots and load with the overload flag, the
satchel's contents, the seven upgrade tiers, the Core Sample's timer, and the
scanner's lock and target: the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep each short
enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the
overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without
changing it. Keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the status bar.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Deepcore's part. Each source is called
with the state current at the read, as `DeepReadonly<DeepcoreState>`, and reads off
that argument. Drawing the panel, toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the
engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Deepcore's part, done through the world's
diagnostics in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of no
arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the world
the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the panel and
toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
